"""Generate, check and name the probe worlds the conformance suite reads.

  tools/probe-worlds generate --accept-eula [--shard NAME] [--only GLOB]
  tools/probe-worlds verify [--shard NAME] [--only GLOB] [--fixtures DIR]
  tools/probe-worlds shards        the CI shards, one per line
  tools/probe-worlds key SHARD     `key=<Actions cache key>` for one shard (CI)
  tools/probe-worlds keys          `<shard>=<Actions cache key>` for every shard (CI)
  tools/probe-worlds inventory     the table below, as JSON
  tools/probe-worlds lint          the table against tools/analysis and ci.yml

UNITS is the list: each unit is one generator command under tools/analysis,
the corpora it writes and the seed each must record, and the CI shard it
runs in. CI runs each shard on its own runner, one server at a time, and
caches it under `key`; so this table IS the set of corpora CI provides
(SPEC §7). A case that reads a corpus no unit writes skips in CI, and CI
fails on any skip tests/conformance/expected-skips.txt does not list.

`generate` replaces each selected unit's corpora whole, runs every selected
unit even after one fails, then runs `verify` on the selection. A unit's
corpora are moved aside to probes.previous/ while its generator runs:
deleted when it succeeds, put back when it fails, so a failed regeneration
(an exhausted heap, a settle timeout, a bad jar) leaves what was there
rather than nothing. A run killed outright leaves them in probes.previous/,
where the next `generate` of that unit takes them as the previous copy.
Because the corpus is out of the way, density-probe.sh's refusal to
replace another seed's corpus does not arise here: the table names the
seed. `verify` starts nothing: every corpus present, recorded
under the seed its cases expect, frozen where its generator freezes, and a
non-empty region for every entry its spec names. The cases cannot be left
to notice, because several score whatever subset of seeds or entries they
find, so a half-generated set would pass them.

A shard's cache key hashes its rows of this table and the text of every
script those rows run (each generator and the tools/analysis/*.sh it
calls, i.e. density-probe.sh): editing one generator regenerates only its
shard. The engine is never an input; the server's output does not depend on
it. FORMAT, or PROBE_CACHE_EPOCH in ci.yml, forces a regeneration for a
change this cannot see (the JDK patch level, the heap, the runner).

Running a generator runs the Minecraft server, so `generate` needs
--accept-eula, which it passes on to each generator and accepts for nobody
else. Nothing this writes is committed or uploaded: worlds are
Mojang-derived (SPEC §12).
"""
